  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 319 PM EDT Thu Sep 10 2026\n\nAfternoon GOES satellite imagery reveals mostly sunny skies across \nthe northern Great Lakes and Upper Midwest aside from diurnal cu \nlocally seen in the interior UP. With ridging aloft holding through \ntonight, clear skies should allow temps to fall into the low 50s and \nupper 40s for much of the interior while the lakeshores remain in \nthe 50s. A breakdown of the ridge aloft to more zonal flow and mid-\nlevel troughing shapes up across the central CONUS tomorrow morning, \ndeveloping a surface low in the northern Plains and increasing the \nregion pressure gradient across the Great Lakes. Southerly return \nflow will make more another warm summer day with highs near 80 (a \nbit cooler near northern Lake Michigan). A cold front finally \nreaches the western UP late tomorrow night, pressing into the \neastern 2/3rds by Saturday morning. Showers and a few thunderstorms \nare expected along the frontal boundary, but with waning \ninstability, no strong to severe storms are anticipated. HREF QPF \nprobs for only a 0.10\" are nothing to write home about, only peaking \nat 50-70% in a stretch from Ironwood to Houghton. Eastward, light \nrain amounts a tenth of an inch or less is expected with showers \ndissipating past the central UP. Modest pressure rises and cold air \nadvection will make for a windy but dry day on Saturday. Cant rule \nout some scattered lake effect rain showers or at least clouds on \nSunday as 850 mb temps fall below 5C (0-2C if you're the Canadian or \nGFS) while the lake average surface temp sits at ~17C.\n\nLooking into early next week, ensembles continue to hone in on a \ndeepening trough over the Rockies ejecting a Colorado Low in the \nvicinity of the Great Lakes. There remains considerable disagreement \nbetween each ensemble and their individual ensemble members \nregarding the track and strength of the surface low pressure. Given \na broad feature placement north and west of the UP, considerable \nmoisture (PWATS near 1.75-2\", the 95-99th percentile of \nclimatology), if the best QPF axis winds up in our neck of the woods \nthen meaningful rainfall could be expected. The NBM suggests a 60-\n80% chance for QPF >0.5\" by Tuesday morning, however, this may be \nhigh biased as individual ensembles and the LREF barely scrape 20-\n40%.",
